The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For any $p > 1$, we analyze the $\ell_p$ norm of $x_n - c$. Since $\|x_n - c\|_1$ is small, each term $|x_n(k) - c(k)|$ is bounded by this small value. For $p > 1$, $|a_k|^p \leq |a_k| \cdot \|a\|_1^{p-1}$ (as $|a_k| \leq \|a\|_1$), leading to $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$. Thus, $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$, proving convergence in $\ell_p$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

