The symmetric group $ S_n $ always has normal proper subgroups. For $ n \geq 3 $, the **alternating group $ A_n $** (consisting of all even permutations) is a normal proper subgroup. This is because conjugation in $ S_n $ preserves the parity of permutations, ensuring $ A_n $ is closed under conjugation. For $ n = 2 $, the trivial subgroup $ \{e\} $ is the only proper normal subgroup. Thus, such subgroups exist for all $ n \geq 2 $.

**Final Answer:**  
\boxed{A_n}

The matrix $ Q $ has orthonormal columns, so $ Q^T Q = I_n $, where $ I_n $ is the $ n \times n $ identity matrix. However, $ QQ^T $ results in an $ m \times m $ matrix. Since $ m > n $, $ QQ^T $ cannot equal the $ m \times m $ identity matrix because its rank is at most $ n $, while the identity matrix has rank $ m $. Additionally, $ QQ^T $ acts as a projection onto the column space of $ Q $, which is a subspace of $ \mathbb{R}^m $, not the entire space. Thus, $ QQ^T \neq I $.

**Final Answer**
