The existence of a vector **v** such that $\{\mathbf{v}, T(\mathbf{v}), \ldots, T^{n-1}(\mathbf{v})\}$ is linearly independent implies that **T** is cyclic, meaning its minimal polynomial equals its characteristic polynomial. For such transformations, the Jordan canonical form must consist of a single Jordan block per eigenvalue (if the characteristic polynomial splits). This ensures that each eigenvalue has geometric multiplicity 1, i.e., the eigenspace for each eigenvalue is one-dimensional. Thus, every eigenvalue has a unique eigenvector up to scalar multiplication.

**Answer:**  
